      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{{e}^{5x+2}}{5}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=5x+2$, then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{5x+2}}{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{u}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\cdot {e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{5x+2}}{25}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
